// Formative practice quiz for turning-off-the-dev-profile. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/auth-and-review/turning-off-the-dev-profile

::turning-off-the-dev-profile o1 - risk::What is the danger of leaving the dev profile active on a shared server?{
~The server runs a little slower#Not quite. The issue is exposure, not speed.
=Anyone can call its endpoints with no credentials#Correct. That profile permits all requests, wherever it runs.
~The front end stops compiling#Not quite. A back-end profile does not affect the front-end build.
~None, since a dev profile only works on localhost#Not quite. A profile does not check where it is running.
}

::turning-off-the-dev-profile o2 - starting without::How do you start the back end without the dev profile?{
~Delete the login page#Not quite. That changes nothing about the server.
~Set a VITE variable#Not quite. VITE variables configure the front end, not the back end.
=Remove dev from the active Spring profiles wherever it was set#Correct. Without it in the active list, its open security configuration is not used.
~Log out of the browser#Not quite. Your browser session does not change the server profile.
}

::turning-off-the-dev-profile o2 - vite prefix::Which environment variable does Vite expose to your front-end code?{
~API_URL#Not quite. Without the prefix, it is not exposed to client code.
~REACT_API_URL#Not quite. That prefix is not used by Vite.
~SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE#Not quite. That one configures the back end.
=VITE_API_URL#Correct. Only the VITE_ prefix is exposed through import.meta.env.
}

::turning-off-the-dev-profile o2 - no secrets::Why must a VITE_ variable never hold a secret?{
=It ends up in the bundle that every browser downloads#Correct. Client code is public, and so is anything bundled in it.
~Vite encrypts it badly#Not quite. Vite does not encrypt it at all. It becomes plain text in the bundle.
~Secrets make builds slower#Not quite. Speed is not the issue.
~It is fine when the variable name is long#Not quite. The name does not protect the value.
}

::turning-off-the-dev-profile o3 - verify::How do you verify the switch is effective?{
~Open the app while signed in#Not quite. A signed-in browser works in both cases, so this proves nothing.
~Read the configuration file only#Not quite. That shows intent, not what is actually running.
~Check for a 200 response#Not quite. A 200 with no credentials means it is still open.
=Send a request with no credentials and confirm a refusal, then confirm the front end handles it#Correct. This tests the protected state directly on both sides.
}
